# One command, for the desktop shortcut that turns SSH on.
#
# The shortcut is tapped on a touchscreen. Asking to authenticate there means
# raising the on-screen keyboard over a modal dialog to type a password that is
# printed in a file on the same desktop, which buys nothing; and the person
# tapping it is already in the sudo group, so they can run the same systemctl
# by hand. What this removes is the prompt, not the authority.
#
# The grant is one absolute path with one fixed argument. --apply is the half
# of chiron-ssh-enable that enables and starts ssh.socket and does nothing
# else; the script rejects it unless it is root, so this is the only way it is
# reached. Any other invocation, and every other command, still asks.
#
# polkit would be the other way to do this, and is not used here: on this board
# it has been the fragile part -- the agent registers per session, the helper
# has to fall back to setuid because the vendor kernel has no pidfd -- and a
# rule that quietly fails to apply produces exactly the password prompt this
# exists to avoid. sudo has no session to be registered in.
%sudo ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/local/bin/chiron-ssh-enable --apply
